<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kisaeng</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ginyeo, gisaeng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kisaeng, also called ginyeo, were enslaved women from outcast or enslaved families in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> who were trained as courtesans to provide artistic entertainment and conversation to upper-class men. They emerged under the Goryeo dynasty and served the state at court and in the provinces, trained in music, dance, poetry and prose; some also worked in medicine and needlework, and many sijo by kisaeng survive. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheonmin">cheonmin</a> status was hereditary, most careers were over by age 22, and in 1650 all kisaeng were made slaves of the government. The Gabo Reform of 1895 made them nominally free, and under Japanese rule the Kisaeng Regulation Order of 1908 grouped them with licensed prostitutes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kisaeng.jpg" alt="Kisaeng" width="295" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ginyeo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Kisaeng, also called ginyeo, were enslaved women from outcast or enslaved families who were trained as courtesans to provide artistic entertainment and conversation for men of the upper class. The origin of the kisaeng is unclear. A theory linking them to the Silla wonhwa, female predecessors of the hwarang, has little support, since the wonhwa seem to have been aristocrats. Many scholars instead trace them to the early years of Goryeo, when many people were displaced after the end of the Later Three Kingdoms period in 936. The first king of Goryeo, Taejo, considered these wanderers a threat and ordered them made slaves of the government, and the first kisaeng were probably drawn from them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kisaeng emerged as a class during the Goryeo dynasty and are first mentioned in the early 11th century, when they were engaged mainly in skilled trades such as needlework, music and medicine. Training academies called gyobang were set up for court entertainers, and they entertained the king and visiting dignitaries and, from the reign of Munjong, performed at state ceremonies.</p><p>The system continued under the Joseon dynasty, although the Confucian scholars of the time took a dim view of professional women. Proposals to abolish the kisaeng failed. Under Yeonsangun, between 1494 and 1506, kisaeng became symbols of royal excess: he brought 1,000 women and girls from the provinces to serve as palace kisaeng. In 1908 the Kisaeng Regulation Order grouped them with licensed prostitutes under Japanese rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Kisaeng held the status of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheonmin">cheonmin</a>, the lowest in society, and it was hereditary, so that daughters of kisaeng became kisaeng. Women also entered the class by being sold by families who could not support them. A kisaeng could be released only by payment of a heavy price to the government, usually by a wealthy patron. Most careers were short, peaking at 16 or 17 and generally over by 22, and all kisaeng had to retire at 50. Many former kisaeng worked in or managed a local tavern.</p><p>In later Joseon a three-tiered system developed, with the ilp'ae who sang and danced at upper-class feasts at the top and the samp'ae at the bottom. Kisaeng were trained in music, dance, poetry and prose, and many sijo composed by them survive. Kisaeng also acted as sources of intelligence, and some, such as Nongae of Jinju, are remembered for killing or attempting to kill Japanese commanders during the invasions of 1592-1598.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kisaeng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gisaeng</b>, the female entertainers of the court and local offices, were government nobi.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
